<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/expert/ethics-of-technology-and-ai">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Ethics of technology and AI is philosophy's newest applied branch — and its most urgent:
    <strong>systems that make decisions about people demand answers to questions philosophers
    have worked on for centuries</strong> (responsibility, fairness, moral status, autonomy) at a
    pace the tradition never anticipated. This lesson maps the questions and the emerging
    answers.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="alignment as ethics, machine moral status, responsibility gaps, and power" widget="equationStep" config={techCfg} />

  <h2>The frameworks in use</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Principlism scaled up:</strong> the OECD/UNESCO AI principles (transparency, fairness, accountability, human oversight) — Kantian deontology, consequentialist impact assessment and virtue-institutional design, drafted as policy.</li>
    <li><strong>Meaningful human control (Dutch drones debate):</strong> a concrete standard — humans must have the information, time and authority to intervene meaningfully. Adopted in EU AI discussions; testable, unlike vague "oversight".</li>
    <li><strong>The precautionary vs proactionary debate:</strong> regulate risks before proof of harm vs innovate and fix later. Both cite real cases (asbestos vs overregulated early internet). The honest answer is domain-specific thresholds — which is what regulators are now writing.</li>
    <li><strong>Value-sensitive design (Friedman):</strong> embed values at the DESIGN stage — stakeholder analysis, value scenarios, technical requirements. The most operational bridge between philosophy and engineering practice.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> UNESCO Recommendation on the Ethics of AI (2021); EU AI Act (2024); Friedman & Hendry, Value Sensitive Design (2019); Vallor, Technology and the Virtues (2016).</p>
</TopicPage>
